
Colonial Pipeline Ransomware Incident: Cyber Law, Ethics, and Security Analysis

This report analyzes the Colonial Pipeline ransomware attack, focusing on legal, ethical, and security failures that enabled the breach. It evaluates MFA gaps, legacy VPN exposure, NIST compliance issues, and global regulatory impacts. The report concludes with actionable recommendations aligned with modern cybersecurity frameworks.



Introduction

Technology has been advancing at an exponential rate. The number of online businesses has skyrocketed. Due to this change more sensitive data such as credit card information and social security numbers are online. This increase has led to the increased need for cybersecurity.
	The purpose of these cyber principles is to provide guidance for businesses to keep this sensitive data safe. When it comes to people’s personal information there are both legal and ethical issues to deal with. Cyber laws were established to make sure that people feel safe when they shop on the internet. These laws are in place to allow access to authorized users and block those that are unauthorized. There are also times when I authorize a company that I’m buying something from to see my credit card information. When this happens, I expect it to only be looked at long enough to complete this transaction.
	There are a lot of types of cybercrime that we must look out for in today’s world. Phishing, hacking, identity theft, and ransomware are all major issues.  By having cybersecurity standards, the average hacker will be far less likely to gain access to sensitive data. Each organization is required to maintain these standards or face legal consequences. The purpose of cyber principles is to keep data safe.
	I chose the Colonial Pipeline incident for my case. This happened in May of 2021. A hacker group that called themselves DarkSide hacked into the network and stole 100 gigabytes worth of data. Then they installed ransomware onto several systems preventing the organization from being able to access them. The accounting and billing computer systems were both compromised. 
	I chose this because the Colonial Pipeline delivers oil that is needed for gasoline. This gasoline is used for heating houses and for jet fuel.  The fact that this oil wasn’t being delivered was disastrous for millions of people who couldn’t get gas or had to pay way more for gas than usual. The pipeline was down for 5 full days and Colonial Pipeline paid 75 bitcoins for the password to access their systems to get the pipeline going again. This was worth over 4 million dollars at the time.
	The application of cyber principles is meant to stop this kind of thing from happening. When hackers are caught, they face the cyber laws that are in place to deal with this kind of thing. This Colonial Pipeline incident only lasted about 6 days but caused a major disruption to the country. We must continue to improve these laws and principles to stop this kind of thing from happening.
Case Analysis

Colonial pipeline had the ethical responsibility to ensure that it had up to date security measures so that it could keep users’ data safe. The company didn’t keep the security standards that I would expect from any company. The lack of certain security measures is not only an ethical issue, but also might be a legal issue.
	Remote access to sensitive information requires that there be multifactor authentication in use. Multifactor authentication is required for certain industries because of the amount of personal information these companies have. The National Institute of Standards and Technology, also known as NIST highly recommends using MFA for protecting businesses of all sizes. They also recommend using the rule of least privilege. This would only allow hackers minimal access if they got their hands on someone’s account. Colonial pipeline was also lacking a policy that would ensure employees have unique passwords. The information isn’t publicly available, but it’s speculated that an employee’s password was compromised in a different data breach which allowed the hackers to gain access to colonial pipelines. All these ethical and legal issues may have been caused by the company not wanting to spend the extra money on security. 
This is where a risk analysis should have come into play. The data breach ended up costing them 4.4 million dollars. It also caused a huge panic in the southern United States. If they had done the risk analysis, they probably would have realized that having the extra security would have cost them less in dollars and in reputational damage.
The hack was deemed a national security threat, as the pipeline moves oil from refineries to industry markets (Kerner, 2022). This attack didn’t seem to be targeted at any demographic. It’s possible that the hackers were doing this because they have a grudge against the United States and wanted to cause issues with our infrastructure but since it was a ransomware attack it seems to me that the hackers most likely did it for the money. 
	Hackers that are trying to hurt certain people or certain groups probably would have tried to destroy the companies’ systems. They also may have advertised why they chose to hack colonial pipelines or announced that they were doing it to hurt a certain group. The lack of any of these things are other reasons why I believe Darkside wasn’t trying to hurt any specific demographic groups. The lack of compliance with certain standards of security caused panic for a few days as well as the monetary loss.
	This attack had a major impact on society as well. Hackers were around but not a lot of people had experienced firsthand what a hacker could do. In this case the hackers were able to stop flights and stop houses from having oil to use as heat. This was probably a lot of people’s first real experience seeing what these attacks could do. This caused a lot of fear in the public around the area that this occurred. If hackers could turn off the pipelines what else could they do?
Incident Impact
	This incident caused a major change in IT regulations. The hack was such a big deal that President Joe Biden said it was a national security threat. The company, Govtech, points out that multifactor authentication is something that should have been standard when this happened. In the case of the Colonial Pipeline cyber-attack, MFA could have prevented the hacker from gaining access to its internal systems and threatening to stop production of the fuel pipeline (GovTech, 2021). 
	In May 2021, President Joe Biden signed an Executive Order forcing the government to “adopt security best practices [and] advance toward Zero Trust Architecture” (Savage, 2022). Part of this was making multifactor authentication a requirement for any type of government agency. I believe that simple process of having to use at least two ways to check someone’s identity would have saved the government from having issues such as the one caused by the colonial pipeline attack. This belief comes from the fact that the president had declared this incident a state of emergency and then in a short period of time as far as the workings of the government go multifactor authentication started to be a law.
	There are constant threats of cyber-attacks to both companies and to the US government. I was unable to find any specific event but there are always rumors of different countries trying to hack into things for political and monetary gain. We just had a presidential election, and I have seen rumors of China and Russia both trying to hack into media to try and influence elections. This kind of thing will certainly affect the cyber security laws going forward and probably have in the past as well.
	Cyber Security attacks have gotten much worse in the last several years due to several factors. More stuff is being put online such as businesses and the ability to bank and shop online. Artificial intelligence and machine learning have also come a long way, and this is something that cybercriminals can use as a resource to help them hack into systems. The standards for cyber security in the past were like strong suggestions that a lot of companies didn’t follow. The Transportation Security Administration (TSA) had suggested voluntary best practices standards to pipeline companies, but even physical security assessments had no enforcement capability (Wood, 2023). A lot of people and companies probably have a mindset where they think it won’t happen to them. This mindset has cost companies millions of dollars.
	These standards should have been made into law a long time ago. The fact that companies keep getting hacked that failed to implement these standards is of major concern. Colonial Pipeline didn’t implement several of these standards such as multifactor authentication and that caused them to be vulnerable to hackers. 
	The Colonial Pipelines attack had a huge cultural impact on cyber security standards that affect both cybercommunication and commerce. They allowed a lot of people to work remotely at the time. This means that a lot of video chats and emails were sent by these workers. These communications can be watched by hackers if they are not properly secured. The exact way these hackers got a hold of an employee password is thought to be via an out of use VPN that wasn’t deleted and was no longer being kept secure but could have also been stolen during some other sort of cybercommunication. 
	Since this attack was deemed a state of emergency it got a ton of media coverage. People and companies all over the world saw this news and some surely used this information to try and harden their own defenses.  The school I work at got hacked into and I know over the years they have updated cyber security based on mistakes they have seen other companies make such as colonial pipelines. Any major event that receives wide media attention will have a cultural impact. The views of companies now are probably more focused on protecting personal data than they were before this happened.
Recommendations and Global Considerations
Recommendations
	When Colonial Pipelines got hacked a lot of people were asking, could we have prevented this from happening? The answer to this is yes it could have probably been prevented with the proper security measures. DarkSide was able to infiltrate and take over Colonial Pipeline’s network by using a single leaked password and username combination that allowed them to simply log in to a “legacy VPN” that was still active within the system (Walborn, 2021). Legacy systems are almost always vulnerable. This is because they are no longer being updated. When this happens, hackers have much longer to break into a system because it is no longer being changed.
	They should have also had an additional security measure such as multi-factor authentication. This simple security measure would have prevented the data breach by requiring the hackers to have something such as an employee’s cell phone to get a call or text to prove that the hackers should be allowed into the system. Having to actually go and steal a phone is much more dangerous than trying to hack into a computer system from the other side of the world. 
	There are several other basic security measures that may have been able to prevent this data breach from happening. Creating strong passwords is a very easy way to stop hackers. Longer passwords that have numbers, letters, and symbols slow down hackers that are trying to brute force a system. They could also have a policy that requires cookies to be deleted every Friday. Cookies carry information from websites that hackers may be able to access. This information may allow them to figure out passwords easier. 
	Two years have passed since the Colonial Pipeline incident, but not enough has been done to thwart future attacks like it (Industry Dive, 2024).  One ethical guideline that should be in place is to make sure that system software is updated regularly to prevent breaches. Customers data should be protected at all costs and not updating systems is a major hazard that could be fixed. Today multi-factor authentication is a regulation in a lot of industries including government and finance services. Both should go a long way to prevent future data breaches.
Global Considerations
	The most recognized international standards for information technology would be the ISO 27001. The ISO 27001 framework is a set of requirements for defining, implementing, operating, and improving an Information Security Management System (ISMS), and it is the leading standard recognized by the ISO for information security (Kosutic, 2024).  This framework was designed to be cost effective and be able to secure companies of all sizes information. This works by finding out what type of risks these companies might face. Once these risks are found they are dealt with by either accepting them, transferring them, mitigating them, or avoiding them.
	They do this with a series of security controls. The first of these controls deal with organization. They state how software and users are supposed to behave and notice when they don’t behave according to standards. This way, if a user or software acts strange it can be investigated to see what’s going on with it. The ISO 27001 also has controls for people. This is basically a way to make sure that people are being trained to do their jobs in a safe way that doesn’t compromise security. There are also physical controls such as cameras and locks that help keep information from being stolen physically. Finally, there are technological controls that make sure the software is up to date. Technological controls also recommend companies have backups of important data and good antivirus software.
	 All these standards would have helped with the Colonial Pipeline data breach. Having people that are properly trained would have helped employees know not to use weak passwords or the same password for multiple accounts across various platforms. The Technological controls probably also recommend having things like multi-factor authentication which would also have stopped this data breach from occurring.
	This incident had a huge impact on global communication and commerce. Airlines were without fuel for several days causing hundreds of flights to be cancelled. Anybody with business to attend somewhere they couldn’t drive had to reschedule if possible. The airlines lost millions of dollars during these days without fuel. There were also houses that were unable to use heat during this time because they were also gas powered.  Because of this incident cyber security standards were changed for all government organizations in the United States to try and keep something like this from happening again. Other countries also had to change how they thought about cyber security. Hackers could now do something that would have a major impact on global commerce. This could not be allowed and so things that were not thought of as important such as multi-factor authentication are now thought of as something that everyone should be doing.
	The world now has a culture of doing everything online. We share photos and information online for the world to see. We also do a lot of shopping on the internet. Most people at the time probably would buy stuff from a website without even worrying about whether their information was safe. After this event that affected so many people the culture of ecommerce was affected because now people are afraid of what hackers could do. This is a huge ethical issue as well. Hackers feel safe stealing stuff online where they do not think they will be caught but probably wouldn’t walk into a bank with a gun and hold up the teller. The fact that they are disconnected from the people makes it easier to steal because they might feel like they are not hurting anyone. If they were ethical people, they would understand that stealing people’s data is just as bad as pulling a gun on someone. 
	This case had many global legal and regulatory impacts on information technology. The United States government declared a state of emergency and shortly after that Software Bill of Materials was born. This is a list of all the people that helped work on software. This way if there is something wrong you can easily see who worked on the software and see if that’s where the issue may be coming from. The National Institute of Standards and Technology recently released an updated cybersecurity framework. This was partly due to the attack on Colonial Pipelines.
	The NIST originally had five functions in the framework. These include identify, protect, detect, respond, and recovery. At the time these were thought to be enough to deal with any kind of cyber incident. The impact that the Colonial Pipelines attack had on America and the world was enough for a sixth function to be included into the NIST. This new function is called Govern and is there to make sure that companies have an organized way to deal with cyber incidents. Cyber Security is now of huge importance due to the growing number of things connected to the internet. 

Summary

	I applied cyber law principles by explaining how they are used for data protection, privacy, and ecommerce. For data protection I showed how Colonial Pipeline could have used practices such as strong passwords and multi-factor authentication to protect its data. It is ethical to do whatever you can to protect user data from hackers. Colonial Pipelines’ business model is to transport gas for home heating and for airplanes. I explained that they did not follow standards set forth by NIST to keep data safe and by doing some of the simple things NIST recommended they would not have had a data breach.
	I also showed that there are laws that protect a user’s privacy while on the internet. The laws were created to keep people’s information safe when they shop on the internet. Ecommerce is growing rapidly, and new laws and regulations need to keep being developed to make sure that users’ data is safe.








